@L2Anders83 McDonald's and Walmart pay their workers the market rate for their labor, otherwise no one would be willing to work there. BTW, both companies hire workers with little to no experience, that would not otherwise be able to easily find other work, and they provide their employees much better benefits than many other employers.

It's actually better for the government to provide low skilled workers additional social benefits than force companies to pay workers an artificially decided on wage. What you don't seem to understand is that companies like McDonald's and Walmart can easily pass additional costs (like higher wages) onto consumers. They can also hire endless amounts of attorneys to find ways to get around any policy the government puts into place. It's small business that really gets hurt when you increase the minimum wage and create additional regulations to "protect workers", because they do not have the resources or leverage to quickly deal with additional costs and regulations.

It's almost always best to let the free market do its thing, and have the government only step in when there is absolutely no other choice.
